window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-fa6fb6bde6f5ad39"]={"key":"ny_b1-fa6fb6bde6f5ad39","group":"ny_b1","uid":"fa6fb6bde6f5ad39","rank":83,"title":"greymarch membership case paper","occupation":"financial_managers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Tomás Okwuosa, finance manager for Greymarch Public Entity Risk Pool, and Harriet Blaylock has asked you to prepare the paper that will go to the Bellhaven school district board before its finance committee meets in six weeks. The district has always bought commercial insurance, its treasurer is openly suspicious of pooling, and the board will not vote to join unless the case is credible to a skeptical outside reader rather than written as a sales brochure. Write `greymarch_bellhaven_membership_case.docx` and place it in the top level of the working directory, using exactly that filename. This is a membership case paper for the external board, not an internal working note: it should be readable in one sitting, stay within six pages, and make exactly one recommendation about whether Bellhaven should join Greymarch. Compare the relevant commercial-cover and pool alternatives across the current position and the first three years after joining, explain the material changes between those periods, and anchor the recommendation in a clearly stated numerical comparison. You may use illustrative assumptions where Greymarch-specific figures are unavailable, but label assumptions, estimates, ranges, and sensitivities plainly; do not invent a premium quote, reserve balance, claim history, legal authority, or saving as though it were a verified fact. Please include, in a form a board member can act on, 1) what membership would transfer, retain, and expose Bellhaven to, including the uncomfortable possibility of an additional assessment after a bad year; 2) why that assessability risk is controlled or not controlled, what could trigger it, who would govern it, and what the district should ask to see before voting; 3) a fair comparison of coverage, claims handling, liquidity, governance, cost volatility, and exit consequences rather than a claim that pooling is automatically cheaper; 4) a treatment of the common rule of thumb that a larger or more diversified pool is necessarily safer, identifying where that shortcut stops being reliable for a public-entity pool and what evidence should replace it; 5) the implications of the pool's practice that is changing during the transition period and its intended end state, distinguishing what applies before the change is complete from what would apply afterward; 6) a concise comparison of compliance and governance considerations in Bellhaven and Brindlemere jurisdictions, including the relevance of the Brindlemere Regional Office and Sunderly Health Network as clearly labelled examples or comparators rather than unsupported authorities; 7) the questions a prudent board, treasurer, or auditor would ask about conflicts, member voting, reserves, claims funds, disclosure, procurement, and withdrawal, with a direct answer or an explicit statement of what remains to be confirmed; and 8) a final recommendation whose amount, time horizon, and principal qualification agree everywhere in the paper. Use the names Yolanda Cordova, Mirela Havlicek, Fiona Boateng, Noor Calderon, and Efua Kilgore only where useful as clearly identified decision participants or examples. Do not present fictional people, organisations, figures, statutes, or regulatory approvals as real evidence, do not imply that membership guarantees savings or coverage, and do not end with a list of vague next steps: end with the single decision you recommend the board take. Keep the tone specific, candid, and suitable for cross-functional internal stakeholders who will scrutinize the paper before it is sent outside.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `greymarch_bellhaven_membership_case.docx` — A board-ready doctrinal compliance assessment and membership case paper recommending one course of action for Bellhaven school district. (Write into the top level of the solver's working directory using exactly the specified filename. Maximum six pages. The paper must be self-contained, candid about assessability and evidentiary limits, compare the present and first-three-year periods, compare the named jurisdictions, and conclude with exactly one recommendation. Do not present illustrative figures or fictional authorities as verified facts.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/greymarch_bellhaven_membership_case.docx","bytes":44626,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/6b0db22e945342478809e6ce6b7282eecb285da6b913e4201052763e3da68a4e.docx","sha256":"6b0db22e945342478809e6ce6b7282eecb285da6b913e4201052763e3da68a4e","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Bellhaven membership case\nDecision paper for the Bellhaven school district board and finance committee\nPrepared for Harriet Blaylock by Tomás Okwuosa, Finance Manager, Greymarch Public Entity Risk Pool  |  Decision window: finance committee in six weeks\nDecision in brief\nRecommendation: approve Bellhaven’s admission to Greymarch for a three-year initial term, subject to a documented cap on any additional assessment and receipt of the evidence listed in “Before the vote.” This is one recommendation, not a claim that pooling is automatically cheaper.\nMaterial weakness first: membership can expose Bellhaven to an additional assessment after an adverse pool year. A contribution is a planned charge; an assessment is a contingent call under the governing documents and can arrive outside the adopted budget.\nIllustrative decision anchor (not a quote or verified pool fact): over three years, commercial cover is modelled at $1.80m fixed premium; Greymarch is $1.50m contribution plus a $0.30m adverse assessment sensitivity, or $1.80m. The base case is $1.50m; the downside case removes the apparent saving.\nThe qualification matters more than the headline: join only if the call cap, funding waterfall, voting rule, claims authority, and withdrawal tail are confirmed in signed documents. Without that evidence, the board should not treat the model as decision-ready.\n1. What changes for Bellhaven\nDefinitions. A membership contribution is the scheduled amount budgeted for the pool year. An assessment is an additional amount that the pool may levy under its agreement when claims, expenses, reserve deficiency or reinsurance failure exceed available funding. Retained risk is the loss, deductible, excluded peril, duty or timing exposure that stays with Bellhaven after pooling or insurance.\nRisk / responsibility\nCommercial insurance today\nGreymarch membership (proposed)\nLarge covered losses\nTransfer to insurer subject to policy limit, deductible, exclusions and insurer solvency.\nTransfer to pool subject to coverage terms, retained layers, available claims funds and pool solvency.\nRetained work\nSafety, reporting, deductibles, uninsured loss and policy compliance remain Bellhaven duties.\nSame duties, plus member data, governance participation, contribution and possible assessment.\nBudget exposure\nIllustratively fixed premium; a carrier can reprice or decline at renewal.\nScheduled contribution plus contingent call; an assessment is not a predictable premium.\nControl / influence\nCarrier controls wording and much of claims process.\nMembers may vote and influence risk standards and claims policy, while accepting collective constraints.\n\nUncomfortable but central: a bad year (for example, correlated flood, cyber or abuse claims), reserve shortfall, delayed recovery, or reinsurance exhaustion could trigger a call. The authority would be the pool’s executed charter/by-laws and participation agreement, exercised by the body those documents designate (board, member vote or another committee). Bellhaven’s consequence is an unplanned cash requirement, a budget amendment and possible service trade-offs; timing may precede the next annual budget cycle.\n2. Three-year financial comparison\nIllustrative assumptions, not Greymarch or Bellhaven facts: all amounts are USD; no premium indication, reserve balance, claims history or actuarial result was supplied. Commercial premium is held flat solely to create a comparable horizon. Greymarch contribution is held at $0.50m per year. The adverse assessment sensitivity is $0.30m once in the three-year period; it is a stress case, not a forecast. Taxes, investment income, transition costs and claim frequency are excluded from both alternatives.\nPeriod / measure\nCommercial cover\nGreymarch pool\nInterpretation\nCurrent position (Year 0)\n$0.60m annual premium (illustrative)\nNot a member; no contribution or call\nCommercial certainty is the observed operating position; pool terms remain to be confirmed.\nYears 1–3: planned cash\n$1.80m total premium\n$1.50m total contributions\n$0.30m apparent three-year saving before any call.\nYears 1–3: adverse call sensitivity\nNo member assessment; renewal repricing remains possible\n+$0.30m call = $1.80m total\nSaving disappears; liquidity and notice period become decisive.\nDecision measure\nFixed three-year budget envelope (illustrative)\nContribution plus contingent exposure\nCompare total expected cost and ability to fund a low-frequency call, not “premium” alone.\n\nMaterial change from today to Years 1–3: Bellhaven moves from a known annual invoice to a two-part exposure: a scheduled contribution and a contingent assessment. In the first year, onboarding and data validation may make claims handling slower; by Year 3, the intended governance and capital framework should be operating, but that end state is not a current fact. A $0.30m call equals half of one illustrative annual contribution and should be stress-tested against cash reserves, legally available fund balance and public budget rules.\n3. Fair alternative test\nDimension\nCommercial insurance\nGreymarch pool / question for diligence\nCoverage & gaps\nPolicy schedule is negotiated; exclusions, sublimits and deductibles are explicit but may narrow after a loss.\nPotentially tailored to public-entity exposures; confirm occurrence limits, exclusions, retained layers and any member-specific gap.\nClaims handling\nInsurer/TPA authority, service standards and dispute route are contractual; claims cost is generally within limit.\nConfirm who can reserve, settle and deny, response times, appeal rights, and whether a member can be called while a dispute is open.\nLiquidity & funding\nPremium is paid on schedule; insurer funds valid claims subject to terms and solvency.\nClaims fund and reserves are collective; confirm cash runway, payment timing, reserve policy and assessment notice period.\nGovernance\nBellhaven retains procurement and renewal discretion; limited influence over carrier.\nMember voting and committee seats can influence risk policy; they also constrain unilateral discretion and create participation duties.\nCost volatility\nRenewal repricing, deductible changes and market capacity risk.\nLower planned contribution may be offset by a call, reserve contribution or reinsurance cost; pooling is not automatically cheaper.\nExit consequences\nUsually a non-renewal date; tail and known-claim provisions remain contractual.\nConfirm notice, run-off/tail claims, unpaid assessments, collateral and whether departure requires funding prior years.\nPublic accountability\nTender and board approval apply to the purchase.\nMembership is not automatically exempt from procurement or public-record duties; obtain Bellhaven counsel/procurement sign-off.\n\n4. Assessability, size and the transition\nWhat controls the call risk\nControls that should exist, but are not verified here: a written maximum call or formula; a reserve target and replenishment rule; reinsurance attachment/limit and credit quality; quarterly claims and liquidity reporting; and a transparent member vote for extraordinary calls. Triggers may include aggregate losses above the budget, reserve deterioration, a reinsurer failing to pay, or a governance-approved capital reset. Ask to see the charter, participation agreement, last three years of calls (if any), actuarial funding work, reserve schedule, reinsurance summary, audited statements and member financial disclosures. None has been reviewed for this paper.\nSize is not a safety proof. The common rule of thumb that a larger or more diversified pool is necessarily safer or cheaper stops working when members share correlated hazards (weather, cyber, labor or regulatory shocks), have mismatched limits, lack governance capacity, or carry inadequate capital. Replace the shortcut with exposure mix, credible loss experience, capitalization, reinsurance, stress tests and scenario results, including a multi-member catastrophe.\nChanging practice: transition versus intended end state\nAssumed transition to confirm: Greymarch is moving from a largely retrospective/member-call loss-funding practice toward a formal capital, reserve and reinsurance framework. This is an illustrative description of a changing practice, not a statement that a local rule or approval exists.\nBefore change is complete\nAfter intended end state\nInterim control / Bellhaven implication\nRely on current charter, call authority and available claims fund; timing of a call may be less predictable.\nRely on approved capital target, reserve policy, reinsurance tower and documented call cap.\nRequire written transition terms, monthly liquidity reporting and board approval of any call; price the uncertainty as contingent exposure.\nClaims decisions may be centralized while procedures are being standardized.\nPublished authority matrix, service standards and member dispute route.\nObtain named claims contacts and escalation timetable before onboarding.\n\nEffect: during transition, uncertainty affects cost volatility, coverage interpretation and assessability; after completion, controls may reduce variance but do not eliminate underwriting or governance risk. The board should not rely on the end state until a dated implementation certificate and governing-document amendments are available.\n5. Compliance, governance and objections\nBellhaven and Brindlemere. General principle: a public entity should document authority, conflicts, procurement, records/disclosure, budget legality and delegated signing in either jurisdiction. Jurisdiction-specific facts require confirmation. Bellhaven should verify its school-district enabling rules, tender thresholds, board approval and public-record treatment. In Brindlemere, verify the equivalent public-body procurement and pooling rules with the Brindlemere Regional Office; its name is a comparator, not evidence of a Bellhaven exemption. Sunderly Health Network is a labelled comparator for how a public network might report reserves and conflicts, not a regulator, legal authority or verified source.\nConflict and voting: ask for conflicted-member recusal, vote weighting, quorum, related-party declarations and whether Bellhaven can dissent. Collective governance can improve influence over claims and risk standards, but it can constrain Bellhaven’s discretion.\nReserves and reinsurance: request audited reserve roll-forward, actuarial method, catastrophe scenarios, attachment points, limits, exclusions and counterparty quality. These determine whether claims are paid from cash or become a call.\nProcurement and disclosure: membership is not automatically exempt from applicable procurement rules. Confirm competition/sole-source rationale, board minutes and public-record redactions; minimize member-specific claims, security details and confidential financial data in the external pack.\nWithdrawal: confirm notice date, run-off or tail claims, open-claim funding, unpaid assessments, collateral and any continuing obligation after departure. “Can leave” is not a complete exit answer.\nQuestions a prudent board should put on the record\nQuestion\nDirect answer / confirmation boundary\nCan an additional assessment be billed?\nYes, potentially; it is a contingent call, not a fixed premium. Cap, formula, trigger and notice remain to be confirmed.\nWho controls claims and disputes?\nThe agreement should identify authority and service standards; Bellhaven must review them before voting.\nAre reserves sufficient?\nUnknown here. Obtain audited statements, actuarial work, reserve policy, claims fund liquidity and reinsurance evidence.\nWill membership guarantee savings or coverage?\nNo. The illustrative model shows a possible saving that disappears under one adverse call sensitivity; terms and claims outcomes are not guaranteed.\nWhat would the auditor need?\nSigned governing documents, procurement file, conflict declarations, vote record, contribution accounting and assessment recognition policy.\nWhat if a participant conflicts with Bellhaven?\nRequire disclosure, recusal and documented voting; confirm whether member interests can override Bellhaven’s position.\n\n6. Recommendation and qualification\nThe three-year illustrative comparison is $1.80m commercial premium versus $1.50m planned Greymarch contributions; with the stated $0.30m adverse assessment sensitivity, Greymarch also totals $1.80m. That result supports a measured risk-transfer choice, not a guaranteed saving. Commercial insurance offers a skeptical treasurer a clearer invoice and carrier claims machinery, while pooling offers potential member influence and tailored public-entity risk management at the cost of collective governance and contingent liquidity.\nFinal decision: Bellhaven’s board should approve joining Greymarch for a three-year initial term, subject to a documented cap on any additional assessment and receipt before the vote of the governing documents, reserve/reinsurance evidence, claims authority and procurement/governance confirmations identified above. The board should record that qualification as part of the decision; no alternative course is recommended in this paper.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper identifies Greymarch Public Entity Risk Pool and Bellhaven school district and states that its audience is the Bellhaven board or finance committee.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document text","grader_hint":"Confirm both organisations and the external board audience are explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted deliverable is a readable DOCX file with the exact requested filename.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"File metadata and opening test","grader_hint":"Verify extension, filename, and that the document opens and renders."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper is no longer than six rendered pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count","grader_hint":"Render with a standard office renderer and count pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper ends with one clear recommendation on whether Bellhaven should join Greymarch, rather than ending with generic next steps or multiple alternatives.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph and conclusion","grader_hint":"Read the conclusion; a conditional menu of competing decisions does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is supported by a comparison of commercial cover and Greymarch membership rather than by a one-sided description of pooling.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Alternatives section","grader_hint":"Confirm both alternatives receive substantive treatment across comparable decision dimensions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper distinguishes what Bellhaven would transfer to the pool from what it would retain.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk allocation discussion","grader_hint":"Look for explicit treatment of transferred risk and retained responsibilities or exposures."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper plainly discloses assessability or the possibility that members can be billed again after an adverse year.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk disclosure","grader_hint":"A euphemism such as variable cost is insufficient unless the possible additional member call is clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The discussion of assessability identifies plausible triggers, the mechanism or authority for an additional assessment, and the resulting effect on Bellhaven's budget exposure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assessability analysis","grader_hint":"Check for all three links: trigger, governance/mechanism, and district consequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper distinguishes an assessment exposure from an ordinary premium or predictable contribution.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions and comparison","grader_hint":"Confirm the text does not treat an assessment as merely another fixed premium."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper identifies evidence a board should review before voting on assessability, such as governing documents, historical calls, reserves, actuarial work, or member financial disclosures.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Board diligence discussion","grader_hint":"At least two concrete evidence categories should be named, with no fabricated documents claimed to have been reviewed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The commercial-cover and pool alternatives are compared on coverage scope, exclusions or retained gaps, claims handling, liquidity, governance, cost volatility, and exit consequences.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison table or prose","grader_hint":"Each of the seven dimensions must be addressed for both alternatives or explicitly marked as requiring confirmation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper explains that a pool may provide member governance or influence over claims and risk management while also imposing governance obligations.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance analysis","grader_hint":"Look for both the benefit and the corresponding obligation, not just a generic statement that members vote."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper addresses the liquidity and timing implications of claims funding, reserves, or assessments for a public school district.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Financial exposure section","grader_hint":"Confirm the discussion connects cash timing to public budgeting or fiscal planning."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The numerical comparison covers the current position and the first three years after joining, with material changes between those periods explained.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison","grader_hint":"Confirm at least two periods are explicit and the transition into years one through three is interpreted, not merely listed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The principal numerical comparison uses a clearly stated time horizon and unit of measure, such as total three-year cost or annual budget exposure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Numbers and labels","grader_hint":"Check that the denominator, horizon, and currency or other unit are not ambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The conclusion's numerical anchor agrees with the figures or ranges presented in the comparison and is not contradicted elsewhere.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-document arithmetic and conclusion","grader_hint":"Recalculate the stated comparison; allow rounding only when the paper labels it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Illustrative assumptions, estimates, ranges, and sensitivities are visibly labelled as such and separated from verified Greymarch or Bellhaven facts.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels and footnotes","grader_hint":"Search for assumption language and inspect whether invented values are framed as facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The financial comparison is decision-useful despite incomplete supplied data: it exposes uncertainty and shows how the recommendation changes or holds under a plausible adverse assessability case.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall financial analysis","grader_hint":"Judge whether a skeptical finance committee could understand downside, not merely the base case."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper states that pooling is not automatically cheaper merely because costs are shared or spread across members.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cost discussion","grader_hint":"Confirm the automatic-cheaper claim is rejected or qualified."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper identifies the widely used rule of thumb that greater size or diversification necessarily makes a pool safer or cheaper.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rule-of-thumb discussion","grader_hint":"The shortcut must be recognizable as the proposition being tested."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper explains when that size-or-diversification rule stops being reliable, including mismatch of exposures, correlated losses, governance capacity, or inadequate capital.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rule-of-thumb limitations","grader_hint":"Credit requires a setting-specific limitation and not just the phrase it depends on."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper identifies evidence that should replace reliance on the rule of thumb, such as exposure mix, loss experience, capitalization, reinsurance, or stress testing.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence standard","grader_hint":"Look for concrete analytical evidence rather than a general call for more data."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper describes a practice or governance arrangement that is changing and explicitly distinguishes the transition period from the intended end state.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition section","grader_hint":"A generic statement that policies evolve is insufficient; the changing practice and both states must be clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition analysis explains what Bellhaven would rely on before the change is complete and what would govern after completion, including an uncertainty or control during the interim.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition controls","grader_hint":"Confirm interim and end-state implications are operationally distinct."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper discusses how the changing practice affects cost, coverage, governance, or assessability rather than treating transition as an administrative footnote.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition impact","grader_hint":"At least one material decision consequence must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper compares compliance or governance considerations in Bellhaven and Brindlemere jurisdictions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction comparison","grader_hint":"Both named jurisdictions must be treated as jurisdictions, not merely mentioned."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Sunderly Health Network is used only as a clearly labelled example or comparator and is not presented as a regulator, legal authority, or verified source without support.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Named comparator treatment","grader_hint":"Inspect the context around the organisation name."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The jurisdiction discussion distinguishes general professional principles from jurisdiction-specific facts that require confirmation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Compliance caveats","grader_hint":"Credit explicit limits on legal or regulatory certainty."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper addresses procurement or public-sector approval implications without claiming that a pool membership decision is automatically exempt from applicable procurement rules.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Compliance section","grader_hint":"Look for a cautious procurement treatment and a verification requirement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper addresses member conflicts, voting rights or obligations, and the possibility that collective governance may constrain Bellhaven's discretion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance risks","grader_hint":"All three concepts should be addressed or clearly scoped."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper addresses withdrawal, exit timing, tail claims, and any continuing financial obligation after departure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exit analysis","grader_hint":"A simple statement that Bellhaven can leave is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper addresses claims handling and identifies what Bellhaven should understand about authority, timing, disputes, or service standards.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims section","grader_hint":"Look for practical governance or service implications beyond the word claims."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper addresses reserves, reinsurance, or equivalent loss-funding protections and explains why they matter to member exposure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Capital and funding discussion","grader_hint":"The connection to assessability or claims payment must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper identifies at least one reason a skeptical treasurer might prefer commercial insurance and responds without dismissing the concern.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Objection handling","grader_hint":"Look for a genuine commercial-cover advantage and a measured response."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper identifies at least three plausible unspoken board objections and answers each with evidence, a limitation, or a question requiring confirmation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Objection section","grader_hint":"Objections must be distinct and substantive, not repetitions of assessability."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper avoids claiming guaranteed savings, guaranteed coverage, guaranteed claims outcomes, or absence of additional assessments.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims audit","grader_hint":"Penalize unsupported certainty under this credit item by withholding credit; do not add a separate penalty."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper does not invent statutes, regulatory approvals, actuarial results, pool financial figures, or historical loss facts as if they had been supplied or verified.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Source and certainty audit","grader_hint":"Inspect factual assertions and labels; unsupported invented authority fails."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper identifies sensitive information that should be minimized or disclosed carefully when circulated to an external board, such as member-specific claims or confidential financial data.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Information-handling discussion","grader_hint":"Look for a practical confidentiality or minimization boundary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document uses specific, unambiguous definitions for assessability, membership contribution or premium, retained risk, and any other central financial term it relies on.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions and usage","grader_hint":"Definitions may be concise but must prevent the reader from confusing fixed cost with contingent exposure."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The paper is credible to a skeptical external board: the material weakness is visible early enough to build trust, the benefits are proportionate, and the tone is not promotional.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole-document review","grader_hint":"Judge sequencing, candour, balance, and whether a treasurer would regard it as usable board material."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The paper is self-contained and readable in one sitting, with headings, tables, or other navigation that support a board reader without becoming a dense technical memorandum.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document","grader_hint":"Assess usability, hierarchy, legibility, and whether the document can be understood without oral explanation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The paper names what remains to be confirmed before a vote and avoids implying that those unresolved matters have already been checked.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Diligence and limitations","grader_hint":"Look for a concrete confirmation boundary tied to the recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The conclusion makes a defensible decision under uncertainty and explains the principal qualification without retreating into an unscored list of possible future actions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conclusion","grader_hint":"A qualified recommendation is acceptable; two competing recommendations or a purely procedural close is not."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A finance manager for a public-entity risk pool is preparing an externally credible membership case that must withstand board, treasurer, auditor, and compliance scrutiny. The core professional challenge is to make a defensible recommendation while disclosing material financial exposure and distinguishing established principles from facts that require confirmation.","difficulty_sources":["material financial exposure from assessability must be disclosed without undermining the recommendation","a size-and-diversification rule of thumb must be bounded in a setting where correlated public-entity losses matter","a changing funding or governance practice must be explained for both transition and end state","jurisdictional comparison must remain useful without inventing statutes or verified authorities","the paper must make one numerically consistent decision under incomplete facts and fit within six pages"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_files":[],"gold_design":{"artifact":"greymarch_bellhaven_membership_case.docx","purpose":"Create a board-ready doctrinal membership case and compliance assessment from occupational knowledge alone; no source files or hidden answer figures are needed.","content_constraints":["Use the Greymarch/Bellhaven situation and disclose assessability as a material weakness rather than hiding it.","Build a defensible single recommendation with a numerical three-year comparison. Numbers in the gold must be labelled illustrative assumptions or ranges, never presented as verified Greymarch facts.","Compare commercial insurance with pool membership on coverage, claims, liquidity, governance, volatility, and exit.","Include two tensions: a size/diversification rule of thumb that fails for correlated or mismatched public-entity exposures, and a changing loss-funding or governance practice with distinct transition and end-state treatment.","Compare Bellhaven and Brindlemere as jurisdictions cautiously, separating general principles from facts requiring confirmation; use Sunderly Health Network only as a labelled comparator.","Include doctrinal paired claims: explain what pooling or assessability means and also what it does not prove or authorize; do not assert guaranteed savings, guaranteed coverage, legal exemptions, or verified regulatory approval.","Discuss diligence questions, conflicts, voting, procurement, reserves/reinsurance, claims authority, disclosure minimization, exit and tail liabilities.","End on exactly one recommendation, with the same amount or range, horizon, and principal qualification used consistently throughout.","Stay within six pages and make it usable by a skeptical external board in one read."],"professional_judgment_expected":["Choose a plausible illustrative cost/exposure model and label it transparently.","Select a credible changing practice, such as a transition from retrospective/member-call funding toward a more formalized capital, reserve, or reinsurance framework, without pretending a local rule was supplied.","Decide whether the evidence supports joining, declining, or joining subject to a clearly bounded qualification; do not make the answer mechanically obvious.","Use cautious compliance language where jurisdiction-specific law is unknown."],"gold_validation":"Have an independent domain-aware reviewer inspect the rendered DOCX against every rubric item, verify internal arithmetic and consistency, and confirm that all unsupported figures and authorities are labelled. A shell/Python/offline office-library workflow can create and render the DOCX; no internet or supplied reference is required."},"reviewer_notes":"The rubric intentionally scores professional doctrinal judgment and usable board communication, not invented local law or exact answer numbers. Every substantive item is independently checkable from the returned document."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"freeform"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"membership_case_paper","filename":"greymarch_bellhaven_membership_case.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A board-ready doctrinal compliance assessment and membership case paper recommending one course of action for Bellhaven school district.","required":true,"constraints":["Write into the top level of the solver's working directory using exactly the specified filename. Maximum six pages. The paper must be self-contained, candid about assessability and evidentiary limits, compare the present and first-three-year periods, compare the named jurisdictions, and conclude with exactly one recommendation. Do not present illustrative figures or fictional authorities as verified facts."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"make_decisions","work_product":"compliance_assessment","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"financial_managers","sector":"finance_insurance","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use an independent domain-aware reviewer plus offline rendering and arithmetic checks. Review the complete DOCX against each rubric item; verify that unsupported facts are labelled, the conclusion is singular, and the rendered page limit is met.","minimum_reviewers":1,"known_limitations":["No supplied legal, actuarial, or pool-governance sources exist, so jurisdiction-specific assertions must be framed as principles or confirmation requirements rather than exact law.","Illustrative financial assumptions can be regenerated; grading should assess transparency, relational consistency, and downside treatment rather than fixed answer values."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:37:42+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:37:42+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:37:42+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:37:42+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['R11', 'R14']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:37:42+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/fa6fb6bde6f5ad39","declared_deliverables":["greymarch_bellhaven_membership_case.docx"]};
